Hello,

Hopefully I can do this without a flamewar.  Please email replies.

We are engaged in a discussion of USENET news support here at
Mississippi State University.  Some of the people involved are not
familiar with USENET culture and custom.  I've been asked to try to
gather some information about how other universities deal with with
groups that some here feel are "questionable".  I'm already familiar
with all of the logical arguments and documents in favor of a liberal
policy about groups.  But some of the people involved are curious about
"what other schools are doing".  I don't need arguments about why we
should carry these groups but information about who is carrying these
groups.  Also, let me note for the record that we aren't considering
dropping ANY groups...we are considering adding groups that we
currently do not carry.

So, if you are the administrator of a news server in a university
environment I'd be very grateful if you could answer some or all of the
following questions.  If I post a summary I'll eliminate all mention of
particular individuals and institutions.  If you already know of such a
summary available elsewhere please do let me know.

The questions:

1) Are you at a public or private university?

2) Is your news server a departmental or campus wide resource?

   a. If departmental, what department?

3) Roughly how big is your news spool directory/filesystem?

4) Do you carry any alt groups?

5) Do you carry alt.sex groups?

6) Do you carry alt.binaries groups?

   a.  If yes, do you specifically carry alt.binaries.pictures groups?

7) if the answer to any of 4-5 is no, is the restriction primarily
   based (in your opinion) on resource limitations or on concerns
   about the groups' contents?  (remember, I won't be using anyone's
   name or institution in reporting results...so please be honest)
--
Frank Peters  -  UNIX Systems Programmer  -  Mississippi State University
